# Keyhac Microsoft Store (MSIX) identity configuration (template).
#
# Copy this file to `store.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in your values:
#
#     copy store.env.example store.env
#
# build_msix.ps1 loads store.env automatically, so you don't have to pass the
# three identity flags on every pack. Anything passed explicitly on the command
# line (-IdentityName / -Publisher / -PublisherDisplayName) overrides what's here.
#
# NOTE: None of these is a secret -- the three identity values are embedded in,
# and readable from, every published package, and the product ID is the app's
# public Store address. They are kept out of git because they are tied to one
# Partner Center account, so a fork must supply its own rather than silently
# inheriting ours. Without this file the build falls back to a `Keyhac.Prototype`
# identity that packs and sideloads fine but cannot be submitted.
#
# Find all three in Partner Center under your reserved product ->
# Product management -> Product identity ("製品 ID"). Copy them VERBATIM; the
# Store rejects a package whose identity does not match the reservation.

# Package/Identity/Name -- "<PublisherId>.<AppName>", e.g. contoso.Keyhac
KEYHAC_MSIX_IDENTITY_NAME="YourPublisherId.Keyhac"

# Package/Identity/Publisher -- your publisher GUID, including the CN= prefix.
KEYHAC_MSIX_PUBLISHER="CN=XXXXXXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX"

# Package/Properties/PublisherDisplayName -- your display name as shown on the
# Store listing.
KEYHAC_MSIX_PUBLISHER_DISPLAY_NAME="Your Publisher Display Name"

# Store product ID -- the "9N..." id of the reserved listing, shown on the same
# Product identity page (it is also the id in the listing's public Store URL,
# and what `msstore apps list` prints). Not baked into the package; it is used
# by 'make release-windows-msix' to tell the msstore CLI which listing to
# submit the freshly packed .msix to.
KEYHAC_STORE_PRODUCT_ID="9NXXXXXXXXXX"
